{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518507802,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDE0NzEwNjUxMDc="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1518530255,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTI3Mzg2Mw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2158#issuecomment-365273863"},"message":"I think I'm being dense, so apologies for what are probably silly questions.\n\n\u003e such as run --env singularity/conversion_tools\n\nEven with this option, the command executed would still look something like `singularity {run,exec} \u003ccontainer-dir\u003e/singularity/converstion_tools ...`?\n\n\u003e With such info we could then allow rerun to obtain the necessary containers prior execution\n\nBecause containers are in a subdataset, here \"obtain\" is running `datalad get`, right?\n\nIf so, I guess I'm wondering if this (getting a container) could be seen as a more general \"get this input before the run\"\".","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1518532128,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTI4MjY5OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2158#issuecomment-365282698"},"message":"I think it all relates to #2099 #2068 and us just supporting \"runner\" tools which would then take care about collecting needed provenance (be it NICEMAN or anything else like `singularity run`) and we would store that information in the record, while also committing any provenance file generated.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1518532619,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTI4NTAxOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2158#issuecomment-365285019"},"message":"\u003e Even with this option, the command executed would still look something like singularity {run,exec} \u003ccontainer-dir\u003e/singularity/converstion_tools ...?\n\nNot necessarily. The executed command could also be a script, using (possibly several) containers inside, so you can't guess the needed container(s) from looking at the command call.\n\n\u003e If so, I guess I'm wondering if this (getting a container) could be seen as a more general \"get this input before the run\"\".\n\nThat's a good point.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1518532727,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTI4NTUxNw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2158#issuecomment-365285517"},"message":"\u003e Not necessarily. The executed command could also be a script, using (possibly several) containers inside, so you can't guess the needed container(s) from looking at the command call.\n\nThanks @bpoldrack.  That was the (or at least one) obvious thing I was missing :/","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518539680,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NTMyMzE4OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2158#issuecomment-365323188"},"message":"I like the idea of having a general mechanism to label certain paths as \"dependencies\", independent of whether they are containers or not.\n\nOne more idea: We have the situation that we need to run `datalad run` inside a container. Of course that `run` should make a record of the fact that it is running inside a container, so that reproduction is done in the right environment.\n\nSuch behavior can be achieved by providing a `datalad run --interactive`, which actually gives you an interactive shell inside a container. That shell could be prepared with a particular signal environment variable that `datalad run` inspects to see what container it is running on. Otherwise `run --interactive` would work the same way is a non-interactive run, it would still inspect the stay before and after, and commit changes. It would not be able to reproduce such a `run` -- unless `datalad run` is also used inside the container. In that case `run --interactive` exists, finds the dataset clean and nothing is commited. Obviously there is nothing inherently \"interactive\" about this. it could well be a script that executes `datalad run` inside a `datalad run` call that takes place in a container, but an \"interactive\" session might be the easiest method to get the desired behavior.\n\nOf course one needs some kind of gyro to be able to figure out how if you are in a dream within a dream (in a dream, in a ...) -- time really slows in there...","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1519219401,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM2NzMyNDE3OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2158#issuecomment-367324179"},"message":"I'm not quite sure ATM, what we would need that shell for. However, that might be basically the same approach as I imagine.\nFrom my point of view, `datalad run COMMAND` needs to be able to register data-dependencies (that also might be containers used by the command to run) and a container to run the command in.\nAt least for Singularity there's the possibility to pass any ENV variable into the container by prefixing it with SINGULARITYENV_. I guess, there's sth similar for docker.\nThis would allow to make the container known to a command running inside that very container. Since inside the variable doesn't have that prefix (and therefore wouldn't be passed into the next nested container) this mechanism seems to allow for chaining also.\nSo, datalad run COMMAND could watch out for, say DATALAD_CONTAINER_ACTIVE, and register its value as the container to run COMMAND in. Now, if we want to run `datalad run` from within a singularity container, we run that container with SINGULARITYENV_DATALAD_CONTAINER_ACTIVE being set.\n\nSo, if COMMAND itself invokes the container - nothing to do for datalad run. Otherwise use that variable when calling that container (or using it with `singularity run/exec/shell`.\nFor other containers, we just need to figure out, how to set DATALAD_CONTAINER_ACTIVE inside the container and rely on the same mechanic.\n\nDoes that make sense to you guys?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1526657925,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM5MDI0NjkyNg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2158#issuecomment-390246926"},"message":"Addressed by combination of #2464 and datalad/datalad-container#11.","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1526657926,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTYzNDU3MDU0Mg=="},"status":2}]}